{"id":"parallel-and-recurrent-linear-attention","t":"Causal linear attention: parallel and recurrent forms","tags":["Attention","Language Models"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"linear_attention","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Linear attention replaces $\\exp(q \\cdot k)$ with a product of feature maps $\\phi(q)\\cdot\\phi(k)$. The causal output can then be computed two ways that give the same numbers: a **parallel** form with a masked $T \\times T$ matrix (good for training), and a **recurrent** form with a fixed-size state (good for generation, $O(1)$ memory per token).\n\nUse $\\phi(x) = \\mathrm{elu}(x) + 1$ elementwise, i.e. $\\phi(x) = x + 1$ for $x > 0$ and $e^{x}$ otherwise. It is always positive.\n\nWrite `linear_attention(Q, K, V, state=None)`.\n\n## Inputs\n- `Q`, `K`: `float64` `(T, d)`; `V`: `float64` `(T, d_v)`; $T \\ge 1$.\n- `state`: `None` (start empty) or a tuple `(S0, z0)` with `S0` `(d, d_v)` and `z0` `(d,)`: the state left by an earlier chunk of the same sequence.\n\n## Definition\nWith $S_0, z_0$ the initial state (zeros if `state` is `None`):\n$$S_t = S_{t-1} + \\phi(k_t)\\, v_t^\\top, \\qquad z_t = z_{t-1} + \\phi(k_t), \\qquad o_t = \\frac{\\phi(q_t)^\\top S_t}{\\phi(q_t)^\\top z_t}$$\nToken $t$ sees itself and everything before it (including the previous chunks summarised by the state).\n\n## Output\nA tuple `(O_par, O_rec, S_T, z_T)`:\n- `O_par` `(T, d_v)`: computed with matrices and **no loop over time**: a lower-triangular mask of $\\phi(Q)\\phi(K)^\\top$, plus the contribution of the initial state.\n- `O_rec` `(T, d_v)`: computed with the recurrence above, one token at a time.\n- `S_T`, `z_T`: the final state, to pass to the next chunk.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef linear_attention(Q, K, V, state=None):\n    \"\"\"Return (O_par, O_rec, S_T, z_T).\"\"\"\n    A = Q @ K.T                                  # TODO: no feature map, no causal mask, no state\n    O = A @ V / A.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    return O, O, K.T @ V, K.sum(axis=0)\n","hints":["$\\phi(q_t)^\\top S_t = \\sum_{s \\le t} (\\phi(q_t)\\cdot\\phi(k_s))\\, v_s$. That sum is row $t$ of `tril(phi(Q) @ phi(K).T) @ V`.","The denominator is the same sum with $v_s$ replaced by 1: the row sums of the masked matrix. The initial state adds `phi(Q) @ S0` to the numerator and `phi(Q) @ z0` to the denominator.","For the recurrence, update `S += outer(phi(k_t), v_t)` and `z += phi(k_t)` **before** computing $o_t$, so token $t$ sees itself. Copy the initial state so the caller's arrays are not modified."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef _phi(x):\n    return np.where(x > 0, x + 1.0, np.exp(np.minimum(x, 0.0)))   # elu + 1, no overflow warning for big x\n\ndef linear_attention(Q, K, V, state=None):\n    T, d = Q.shape\n    S0, z0 = state if state is not None else (np.zeros((d, V.shape[1])), np.zeros(d))\n    fq, fk = _phi(Q), _phi(K)\n\n    # parallel: causal mask on the (T, T) similarity matrix, plus what the earlier chunks left behind\n    A = np.tril(fq @ fk.T)\n    O_par = (fq @ S0 + A @ V) / (fq @ z0 + A.sum(axis=1))[:, None]\n\n    # recurrent: constant-size state, one token at a time\n    S, z = S0.copy(), z0.copy()\n    O_rec = np.empty((T, V.shape[1]))\n    for t in range(T):\n        S += np.outer(fk[t], V[t])\n        z += fk[t]\n        O_rec[t] = fq[t] @ S / (fq[t] @ z)\n    return O_par, O_rec, S, z\n","ex_md":"## Why the two forms agree\nSoftmax attention needs every $\\exp(q_t \\cdot k_s)$ separately. With a feature map, $\\phi(q_t)\\cdot\\phi(k_s)$ factorises, so the sum over $s$ can be moved inside: $\\sum_s (\\phi(q_t)\\cdot\\phi(k_s)) v_s = \\phi(q_t)^\\top \\sum_s \\phi(k_s) v_s^\\top = \\phi(q_t)^\\top S_t$. The parallel form evaluates the left side for all $t$ at once with a triangular mask; the recurrent form keeps the running sum $S_t$.\n\nTest 4 continues test 3 from its state after 4 tokens and reproduces test 3's last four output rows: chunked processing is exact.\n\n## Complexity\n- Parallel: $O(T^2 d)$ time and $O(T^2)$ memory, like softmax attention, but with large matmuls that use tensor cores.\n- Recurrent: $O(T d\\, d_v)$ time and $O(d\\, d_v)$ memory, independent of context length.\n- Real kernels use the **chunked** form: parallel inside chunks of 64 to 256 tokens, recurrent across chunks, which is what the `state` argument enables.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Forgetting the positivity of $\\phi$: with a plain identity map the denominator can be zero or negative.\n- Updating the state after computing $o_t$, which makes token $t$ blind to itself (an exclusive scan instead of inclusive).\n- Using `np.exp(x)` for all $x$ inside `np.where`: both branches are evaluated, so $e^{50}$ is computed (harmless here) and $e^{1000}$ overflows with a warning (test 7 uses $\\pm 50$ to keep it visible but finite).\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Why does linear attention recall worse than softmax attention on associative-recall tasks? (The state is a fixed $d \\times d_v$ matrix: at most $d$ key/value pairs can be stored without interference.)\n- How do decay gates (RetNet, Mamba-2, GLA) change the recurrence, and how does the parallel form change with them? (The mask becomes a matrix of cumulative decay products instead of ones.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[0.],\n       [0.]])\narray([[0.],\n       [0.]])\narray([[1.],\n       [3.]])","out":"(array([[1.],\n       [2.]]), array([[1.],\n       [2.]]), array([[4.]]), array([2.]))"},{"in":"array([[1., 0.],\n       [0., 1.],\n       [1., 1.]])\narray([[ 1., -1.],\n       [ 0.,  2.],\n       [-1.,  0.]])\narray([[1., 0.],\n       [0., 1.],\n       [2., 2.]])","out":"(array([[1.        , 0.        ],\n       [0.28100109, 0.71899891],\n       [0.6597463 , 0.8707302 ]]), array([[1.        , 0.        ],\n       [0.28100109, 0.71899891],\n       [0.6597463 , 0.8707302 ]]), array([[2.73575888, 1.73575888],\n       [2.36787944, 5.        ]]), array([3.36787944, 4.36787944]))"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ai-engineering-course/model-tradeoffs/sparse-attention/","t":"Sparse attention","c":"AI Engineering"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ai-engineering-course/llm-internals/what-attention-changes/","t":"What attention changes","c":"AI Engineering"}],"related":["scaled-dot-product-attention","mamba-2-gated-state-scan","gated-deltanet-state-update"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0], [0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.0], [0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0], [3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0], [2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0], [2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[4.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, -1.0], [0.0, 2.0], [-1.0, 0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0], [2.0, 2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.2810010925090342, 0.7189989074909657], [0.6597463029986034, 0.8707302004613235]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.2810010925090342, 0.7189989074909657], [0.6597463029986034, 0.8707302004613235]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[2.7357588823428847, 1.7357588823428847], [2.3678794411714423, 5.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [3.3678794411714423, 4.367879441171443], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-0.6517911526116896, -0.17471729232577715, 1.6637239913911968, 0.659147749832255], [-1.6413972945846467, -0.005203264171931977, -0.6234637409883934, 0.14863152325202633], [-1.608187784186389, 0.2417718768768513, 0.23538091873745476, 1.5756260314314627], [0.3166450164719021, 0.5105466616976417, -1.4931166849642326, 2.2527291247240275], [-1.9156455579583005, 1.1018018558224842, -0.3299040738738497, -0.8806465179277969], [-0.6562850109290311, -0.6720146806586559, 0.3801890922489917, -0.11005869